WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER: THE PUBLISHED GCC RELEASE ASSETS
--------------------------------------------------------

This repository also **publishes GNU GCC binaries as GitHub release assets**.
Those assets are **not** Apache-licensed and are not covered by the root
`LICENSE`. They are:

    GNU Compiler Collection (GCC) — GNU General Public License, version 3

For each published toolchain release (e.g. tag `gcc-15.3.0`):

  * the binaries — one per target ISA, each a native bootstrap of the same
    unmodified upstream source (`gcc-<version>-<distro>-x64.tar.zst`,
    `gcc-<version>-<distro>-arm64.tar.zst`, each with a `.sha256` sibling);
  * the **Corresponding Source** required by GPLv3 §6 — the pristine,
    **unmodified** upstream tarball `gcc-<version>.tar.xz`, published as a
    sibling asset on the same release, sha256-verified against ftp.gnu.org
    (`.sha256` sibling). One source covers every ISA binary on the release,
    because no patches are applied;
  * the **build recipe** — `.github/workflows/build-gcc-toolchain.yml` in this
    repository, which is the complete `configure` invocation and build
    procedure used to produce the binaries.

**We do not patch GCC.** If that ever changes, the modified source becomes the
Corresponding Source and ships in its place.

WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, AND NOT JUST A `LICENSE`
----------------------------------------------

A licence file is an artifact, and an artifact is named for what it covers. A
bare Apache-2.0 `LICENSE` at this root would be correct about the tooling and
**misleading about the releases** — a reader could take it to mean the published
compiler is Apache-licensed, which it is not. An artifact that over-reports its
blast radius converts an absent grant into a believed one, so the boundary
travels with the artifact rather than living in a reviewer's memory.

Third-party dependencies resolved by `malf` for downstream builds carry their own
licences; the projected inventories are the per-artifact CycloneDX SBOMs
generated by `sbom_gen.py`.
